# trapsig

Reproducible, leakage-safe classification of trapping/translocation time-series signals,
implemented against the project design (`design/TECHNICAL_DESIGN_AND_DATA_AUDIT.md`).

The pipeline takes raw recordings, detects events, extracts label-blind features and
event windows, and trains a **hybrid** model: Model A (conventional ML on features),
Model B (a compact 1D residual CNN on event windows), and a fixed convex blend of the two.
Everything else in the design's deliverable list flows from that: leakage-safe grouped
cross-validation, a unified metric engine, explainability with mandatory shortcut probes,
conditional few-shot / semi-supervised / novelty / clustering analyses, figures, CSV
rollups, and a PDF report.

**Status: research code (0.1.0).** All demonstrations use clearly labelled SYNTHETIC data.
On your own data every number is exploratory until an independent validation set exists.

## One command

```bash
python3 -m pip install -e ".[dev]"          # core + Model A
python3 -m pip install -e ".[full]"         # + torch (Model B / hybrid), shap (TreeSHAP), reportlab (PDF)

# 1. Check your configuration and data, and see the estimated runtime. Processes nothing.
trapsig run --config configs/custom_dat.yaml --dry-run

# 2. Run everything: check, prepare, train, validate, explain, plot, report.
trapsig run --config configs/custom_dat.yaml

# Optional fast smoke test first (~minutes): 2 repeats, no permutations, few epochs.
#   trapsig run --config configs/custom_dat.yaml --quick

# Score new recordings with the models a finished run saved (sidecar-checked):
#   trapsig predict --run runs/train_<ts> --model logreg --input-dir /path/to/new/dat
trapsig run --config configs/custom_dat.yaml --quick
```

`trapsig run` calls the individual stages in order, so you never have to remember them:

| Stage inside `run` | What it does | Main outputs |
|---|---|---|
| 1 preflight | 26 checks on configuration and data; nothing is processed | `preflight_report.{json,csv}`, `budget_estimate.json`, `metadata.{csv,json}` |
| 2 prepare | manifest, SHA-256s, symlinks, grouped stratified repeated folds | `data/manifests/`, `data/symlinks/`, `metadata.{csv,json}` |
| 3 train | Model A + Model B + prototype head + fusion head + hybrid, repeated grouped CV, baselines, bootstrap, label-shuffle nulls, **feature-group ablation and redundancy report** | `features.csv`, `predictions.csv`, `metrics.csv`, `model_comparison.csv`, `windows.csv`, `ablation.csv`, `feature_redundancy.csv`, `summary.json`, `checkpoints/`, `logs/`, `metadata.{csv,json}` |
| 4 explain | exact linear SHAP / TreeSHAP, Grad-CAM, integrated gradients, occlusion, **baseline-only shortcut probe**, time-shift null | `explainability.json`, `attributions.npz`, `attributions_summary.csv` |
| 4 advanced | few-shot prototypical, confidence-aware pseudo-labelling, novelty, clustering (all gated, off by default) | `advanced_analyses.json` |
| 5 report | figures, one CSV per PDF section, PDF with results + limitations + information gaps | `figures/`, `report_*.csv`, `report.pdf` |

Useful flags on `run`: `--model-set features|deep|hybrid`, `--no-pdf`, `--no-explain`,
`--from-prepared DIR` (reuse an existing prepare run), `--no-stages` (train only).
The individual commands `dry-run`, `prepare`, `events`, and `train` still exist and behave
exactly as before; `run` is simply those plus the downstream stages.
Set `TRAPSIG_DEBUG=1` to get a full traceback when a run fails, in addition to the one-line reason.

### Every run writes its own split record

The folds are shuffled and re-drawn on every run, so the split belongs to the run and not to the
configuration. `metadata.csv` and `metadata.json` are therefore written into **every** run
directory — `dry-run`, `prepare`, and `train` — and the console prints where they went.

`metadata.csv` has one row per **source** file (files in the pool, files outside it, and files that
were skipped), with the manifest identity fields, this run's per-file outcome, and the split:

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `in_cv_pool` | whether the file entered the cross-validation pool |
| `status`, `reason` | this run's outcome for the file (`ok`, `skipped`, `failed`) and why |
| `n_events`, `n_windows`, `model_b_scored` | detected events, usable windows, CNN prediction |
| `fold_rN` | the fold the file belongs to in repeat N |
| `role_rN` | `test` — it is the held-out file when that fold is evaluated (`not_in_pool` otherwise) |
| `trains_in_folds_rN` | the folds of repeat N whose training set the file belongs to |
| `cnn_inner_val_rN` | the folds of repeat N whose inner validation split drew this file |

`metadata.json` adds the run-level provenance: seed, config fingerprint, repeats x folds, class
counts, grouping policy, an explicit statement that the split is re-randomised per run, the role
convention, and (for a training run) the prepared run it read the split from. Neither file is ever
silently overwritten.

### The PDF has a CSV twin for every section

`report.pdf` is the readable form; the same content is written as one CSV per section, in the same
order: `report_data_landscape.csv` (§1), `report_methods.csv` (§2), `report_results.csv` (§3,
model rows plus the gate G7 row and permutation columns), `report_figures.csv` (§4),
`report_explainability.csv` (§5), `report_advanced.csv` (§6), `report_limitations.csv` (§7),
`report_information_gaps.csv` (§8), `report_reproducibility.csv` (§9), `report_ablation.csv`
(§10: feature groups, ablation variants, redundant pairs). Each section heading in the PDF names its
twin, and the CSV keeps the full un-wrapped text of every cell — what wraps across lines on the page
is one field in the CSV. Table cells in the PDF have hard boundaries (fixed column widths,
52 + 60 + 60 mm in §7, §8 and §10): long text wraps inside its own cell and tall rows continue on
the next page instead of being cut.

### The compute budget is measured, not assumed

`--dry-run` times one or two of **your** real recordings on **your** machine and times one
CNN training batch at the window length your configuration will actually produce. It then
reports collection, Model A, permutation-null, and Model B costs separately, each with the
basis it was measured on, plus the resolved window length and the CNN parameter count. For
135 twenty-second 1 MHz files this is typically dominated by collection (a few minutes),
then by the CNN (see the caveat printed with every estimate).

## The architecture

```
raw .dat (1 MHz, 20 s)
   │  notch at the original rate → anti-aliased staged decimation → optional filter
   ▼
analysed signal (10 kHz) + rolling-median baseline + hysteresis events   [label-blind]
   ├──► Model A: 90 label-blind features in 10 groups (noise, time, amp, event, ctx,
   │       spec, tf, acf, shape, morph) — groups chosen by training.feature_groups
   │       → logreg (C by inner grouped CV) + random forest + HistGradientBoosting
   ├──► Model B: fixed-length windows centred on events, baseline-subtracted,
   │       scaled by the TRAINING-fold robust scale
   │       → stem(strided conv) → residual dilated conv blocks → GAP → linear
   │       (conv → GAP → linear is what makes Grad-CAM mathematically valid)
   │       trained with cross-entropy + a metric-learning term (supcon | prototypical)
   │       ▼
   │   ONE frozen GAP embedding per fold, read four ways:
   │       ├─ softmax head ──────────────► cnn1d
   │       ├─ prototypes from the fold's INNER-VALIDATION files ──► proto_cnn
   │       ├─ logistic head on [handcrafted features, mean embedding],
   │       │   fitted on those same inner-validation files ───────► fusion
   │       └─ the embedding itself ──► few-shot, pseudo-labelling, novelty, clustering
   └──► hybrid: fixed convex blend of Model A and cnn1d, model_a_weight in configuration
            files Model B cannot score (no usable event) fall back to Model A and are flagged
   ▼
file-level decisions, aggregated over repeats → metrics, bootstrap CIs, permutation nulls
   ▼
feature-group ablation (same folds, same files) + redundancy report → PDF §10
```

The learning strategies are one architecture, not ten models: a single encoder per fold, one
representation, and every downstream strategy reading it. `docs/LEARNING_STRATEGIES.md` is the
audit — what is implemented, what flows between the components, what contributes to the final
prediction, what is deliberately absent and why.

Rules that make the numbers trustworthy:

- **Splits are file-level** (each file is its own group; no replicate metadata exists).
  Group disjointness is asserted for every repeat. No holdout, because there are no
  replicates to hold out; every summary says so.
- **Nothing crosses a fold boundary.** Imputation, scaling, logreg `C`, CNN early stopping,
  temperature scaling, the class prototypes, the fusion head, and the pseudo-label thresholds are
  all fitted inside training folds — the prototypes and the fusion head on the fold's
  inner-validation files, which the encoder never trained on.
- **A file is scored only where every repeat scored it.** If the fusion head or the prototype head
  could not score a file in some repeat, that file is excluded from the model's metrics rather than
  averaged in over the repeats that did score it, and the exclusion is reported in `summary.json`.
- **Exactly one imbalance rule** is active, chosen by the recorded ratio (threshold 1.5).
- **Model B is compared to Model A on the matched subset** of files Model B can score, and
  gate G7 (`Model B beats or matches Model A`) is decided there with CI overlap assessed.
  When the intervals overlap the report says the models are indistinguishable and prefers
  Model A, per the design.
- **Files are skipped, never dropped.** A recording that is non-finite or flat stays in
  `features.csv` with its reason and is excluded from the models.
- **Anything that cannot be computed is reported as such**, with a reason, never as a number.
- **Features are validated, not accumulated.** The ablation refits the declared model on each group
  alone and on everything minus one group, on the same folds as the headline CV, so the differences
  are paired. Near-duplicate features are reported in `feature_redundancy.csv` and **never
  dropped**: dropping a column on the pooled matrix would use test-fold information.

### Four-branch deep architecture (opt-in, additive)

`quad.enabled: true` (default **false**) trains one integrated model alongside — never instead of —
Model A/B: a learned temporal branch (dilated residual CNN + attention + transformer), a learned
spectral branch on a fixed STFT front end, self-supervised pretraining (contrastive + masked
reconstruction, training folds only), and 38 engineered window features, fused by cross-branch
attention with reported gate weights, with a softmax head and a prototypical few-shot head.
Paired branch ablations (`quad.branch_ablation`), extended metrics (`metrics_extended.csv`),
few-shot episodes with Wilson CIs (`fewshot_episodes.csv`), a 67-entry visualization registry
(`viz_registry_index.csv`), and PDF sections 11–14 come with it. Full design, leakage contract and
honest scope: [`docs/QUAD_ARCHITECTURE.md`](docs/QUAD_ARCHITECTURE.md). Every knob is documented in
`configs/custom_dat.yaml`, including the sampling-rate menu (`sampling.rate_menu_hz`) that keeps
acquisition rate and analysis rate distinct.

### Round 3: raw per-file figures and scientific visualization coverage

When figures are enabled, every discovered recording gets its own original-source full-recording
waveform plot under `runs/<task>/figures/files/<actual-class-label>/<sample_id>_<filename>_raw.png`.
Long traces use a display-only min/max envelope to preserve spikes without loading all plot points;
source samples are not filtered or altered. `figures/files/raw_file_figures_index.csv` records
successes and skipped/failed inputs. The additive registry now has 106 spec-tagged builders (173
total entries), and the per-event measurements are exported to `events_table.csv`. The complete
behavior, scientific caveats, prerequisites, and 168-item coverage interpretation are in
[`docs/VISUALIZATION_ROUND3.md`](docs/VISUALIZATION_ROUND3.md).

### Round 2: the configurable feature registry (opt-in depth, same defaults)

Branch D's engineered vocabulary now comes from `src/trapsig/featreg.py`: **154 registered
candidates** across 11 column families (time stats, energy/amplitude, envelope, dynamics,
peaks/events/change-points, quality, spectral, time-frequency, entropy & nonlinear dynamics,
AR/cepstral/MFCC/multitaper, SSA/EMD/VMD decomposition) plus learned representations as the 12th
family (branches A–C), organised in three tiers — 57 mandatory, 76 recommended, 21 experimental.
Select with `quad.feature_tiers` / `quad.feature_families`; the full registry with names,
descriptions, units and parameters is exported per run to `feature_registry.csv`, and
recording-level summaries (`_rec_mean`/`_rec_sd`) to `recording_feature_summary.csv`. Column
selection is **training-fold only** (`quad.feature_select: variance`), and the legacy 38 ids stay
an exact subset at tiers [1, 2]. Robustness add-ons, all default-off: `triplet_weight`,
`lr_schedule` (cosine/step), `early_stopping_patience`, per-fold checkpoints under
`checkpoints/`, the `open_set_msp_threshold` unknown-class diagnostic
(`quad_open_set_flags.csv`), and Cohen's kappa + micro/macro/weighted averages in
`metrics_extended.csv`. Details and the STFT-vs-wavelet trade-off table:
[`docs/QUAD_ARCHITECTURE.md`](docs/QUAD_ARCHITECTURE.md) §11.

## Deliverables (design §65)

| Deliverable | Where |
|---|---|
| Modular Python project | `src/trapsig/`, one module per concern |
| YAML configuration, strict and validated | `config.py`; unknown keys are rejected |
| Data-audit + dry-run/preflight | `preflight.py` (P01–P26), `ingest.py`, `inspect-dat` |
| Symlink dataset manager + master manifest | `symlinks.py`, `manifest.py` |
| Leakage-safe grouped CV + valid resampling | `splits.py`, `resample.py` |
| LPF / Gaussian / combined filtering | `filters.py` |
| Baseline and detrending analysis | `events.py` (rolling median), features |
| Event detection | `events.py` (hysteresis, ΔI/I₀ with I₀ source recorded) |
| Signal-derived features | `features.py` |
| Conventional ML baseline (Model A) | `train.py` |
| Deep-learning model (Model B) | `deep.py` |
| Hybrid combination | `hybrid.py` |
| Representation / metric learning | out-of-fold GAP embeddings (`advanced.file_representation`), prototypical heads |
| Few-shot learning | `advanced.few_shot_prototypical` (setting (b), K configurable, SKIPPED with reason when K is infeasible) |
| Semi-supervised + confidence-aware pseudo-labelling | `advanced.semi_supervised` (inductive, τ and δ thresholds, skew rejection, ≤ 2 rounds, retrained from scratch) |
| Optional clustering | `advanced.clustering_analysis` (bootstrap Jaccard stability; ARI/NMI as diagnostics only) |
| Novelty / unknown-class detection | `advanced.novelty_detection` (honestly "not evaluable" without an unknown class; leave-one-class-out as a labelled diagnostic) |
| Unified metric engine | `metrics.py` (applicability declared) |
| Automated training logs | `logs/deep_training_log.jsonl` (per epoch: losses, metrics, LR, timings, batch, window length, parameters, device) |
| Checkpoint management | `checkpoints/cnn1d_*_e<epoch>_val<score>.pt` with config fingerprint |
| ML visualization | confusion, ROC, calibration, learning curves, model comparison with CIs, embedding PCA |
| Scientific signal visualization | analysed trace with events, class-mean event window, band power by class |
| Explainability framework + SHAP + temporal attribution | `explain.py`: exact linear SHAP, TreeSHAP, Grad-CAM, integrated gradients, occlusion |
| Mandatory shortcut probes | baseline-only probe, time-shift null (`explain.shortcut_probes`) |
| Model-comparison framework | `hybrid.build_comparison`, `model_comparison.csv`, gate G7 |
| Statistical uncertainty | file-level bootstrap CIs, repeated CV, label-shuffle nulls |
| CSV reporting | one CSV per PDF section (`report_*.csv`), plus `metrics.csv`, `predictions.csv`, `features.csv` |
| PDF reporting | `report.py` → `report.pdf` |
| Dataset landscape documentation | preflight report, `report.pdf` §1, `windows.csv`, `ignored_files.csv` |
| Information-gap analysis | `report_information_gaps.csv`, `report.pdf` §8 |
| Contribution analysis | per-feature mean |SHAP| per class, per-segment temporal attribution |
| Per-run split metadata | `metadata.py` → `metadata.csv` + `metadata.json` in every run directory (dry-run, prepare, train) |
| Reproducibility metadata | `config_used.yaml`, fingerprints, `capture_environment`, seeds recorded everywhere |
| Limitations and safeguards | `report_limitations.csv`, `report.pdf` §7, and this README |

## Scientific safeguards built in

- **Grouped, stratified, repeated cross-validation.** Rows sharing a replicate or session
  always fall on the same side of a split; this is asserted per repeat.
- **File-level grouping requires explicit opt-in** (`allow_file_level_fallback`) because it
  yields optimistic estimates. This dataset has no replicate metadata, so the opt-in is set
  and every output repeats the caveat.
- **Source files are never copied or modified.** Manifests reference originals by path and
  SHA-256; symlinks are verified after creation.
- **Mains notch before decimation**, and anti-alias suppression is measured, not assumed.
- **Event detection is label-blind** and reports detected events, not molecular events.
- **Explanations never choose models.** Explainability runs after the model is frozen.
- **The baseline-only probe is the most important output of explainability.** If pre-event
  baseline alone predicts the class, the model is reading an acquisition artefact.
- **Test-data hygiene by construction.** Synthetic ground truth is written separately and the
  pipeline never reads it.

## Custom `.dat` data

Your recordings are `.dat` files, one 20 s recording per file, with the class given by the
name of the top-level folder that contains it. Folder names are never hardcoded: any folder
name under `source_dir` becomes a class.

```
<source_dir>/
    <class_1>/  recording_001.dat  ...
    <class_2>/  recording_101.dat  ...
```

**Step 1. Identify the binary layout from one real file (read-only):**

```bash
trapsig inspect-dat --file "<source_dir>/<class_1>/recording_001.dat" --rate-hz 1000000 --duration-s 20
```

It lists every layout (dtype, byte order, columns, header size) the file size allows, with
the first values of each. Pick the physically plausible one. The size narrows the choice but
does not decide it, so this needs your judgement. Nothing is written.

**Step 2.** Put the layout in `configs/custom_dat.yaml` under `data.dat`, and set
`data.source_dir`, `sampling.original_hz`, and `expected_duration_s`.

**Step 3.** `trapsig run --config configs/custom_dat.yaml --dry-run`, then without `--dry-run`.

**Replicate IDs.** None exist for this dataset, so each file is its own group and estimates
are optimistic. If replicate or session IDs appear later, add a metadata CSV
(`file,class,replicate_id[,session_id]`), set `data.metadata_csv` and `data.label_from:
metadata`, and set the fallback back to `false`. Nothing else changes.

**Per-file assessment.** Every file is assessed individually: `per_file_summary.csv` keeps
skipped recordings with their reason instead of dropping them, and `ignored_files.csv` lists
files the glob did not match (your `.png` plots and extensionless files).

**Model B windows.** A file whose events all fall too close to the recording edge yields no
window; Model B reports it and the hybrid falls back to Model A for it, flagged in
`predictions.csv` (`hybrid_source`). The label-noise rate (files with zero usable events) is
reported, per the design.

**Testing without your data.** The synthetic generator writes the same layout:

```bash
python3 -m trapsig.synthetic_cli --out data/CUSTOM_demo --format dat --class-folders \
    --duration-s 0.5 --fs-hz 100000
trapsig run --config configs/demo_custom_dat.yaml --quick
```

That configuration uses the file-level fallback on purpose, because synthetic files have no
replicate IDs. Its results test the program; they say nothing about your data.

## Repository layout

```
configs/     custom_dat.yaml (your data), demo_custom_dat.yaml (synthetic)
docs/        METHODS.md (what each stage does and why), ARCHITECTURE.md
src/trapsig/
  config.py     typed strict YAML config; validate_config + validate_modelling
  ingest.py     .dat/.npy/.csv readers with header and content checks
  manifest.py   discovery, grouping, hashing, duplicates, manifest
  symlinks.py   verified symlink split layout with manifest-only fallback
  splits.py     grouped stratified repeated K-fold with leakage assertions
  preflight.py  checks P01–P26
  resample.py   staged anti-aliased integer decimation + verification
  filters.py    LPF, Gaussian, notch; FilterSpec with figure labels
  events.py     robust noise, rolling baseline, hysteresis detector, Ton/Toff
  pipeline.py   per-recording order: notch → decimate → filter → baseline → events
  features.py   label-blind per-file features
  windows.py    event-window extraction; leakage-safe robust scaling
  deep.py       Model B: 1D residual dilated CNN, training protocol, temperature scaling
  hybrid.py     window→file aggregation, blend, comparison framework, gate G7
  train.py      orchestration of Model A + Model B + hybrid
  metadata.py   per-run split record: metadata.csv + metadata.json in every run directory
  explain.py    SHAP / Grad-CAM / integrated gradients / occlusion / shortcut probes
  advanced.py   few-shot, semi-supervised, novelty, clustering (gated)
  viz.py        scientific and ML figures
  report.py     the PDF and its per-section CSV twins
  stages.py     post-training stages wired into the training process
  metrics.py    metric engine with applicability
  runs.py       run directories, environment capture, exclusive writes
  synthetic.py  SYNTHETIC demonstration data with ground truth
  cli.py        command-line interface, including `run`
tests/       212 tests (unit, property-style, end-to-end on synthetic data)
```

## Development

```bash
make dev        # install with dev tools
make check      # ruff + mypy + pytest
```

## Hyperparameters, inference, figures

- `training.hpo` (`none|random|halving`) + `training.hpo_metric/hpo_n_iter/hpo_seed_offset` and
  `deep.hpo_trials` + `deep.hpo_space_*` run a *nested* search inside each outer training fold;
  every trial is a row of `hpo_trials.csv`, failures included, and `summary["hpo"]` aggregates it.
- `training.save_models` (default true) writes each fitted Model A fold pipeline to `models/`
  with a JSON sidecar; `trapsig predict` loads only through that sidecar.
- `figures_index.csv` lists every figure category the run requested with its generation status
  and reason; the data figures have CSV twins (`report_dataset_composition.csv`, ...).
- `requirements.txt` and `environment.yml` pin the executed environment; reportlab, torch and
  shap are optional and their absence is recorded, never silent.

## What is deliberately not implemented

- **Holdout test set and the test-access ledger.** No replicate or session metadata exists,
  so there is nothing to hold out. Every number is cross-validated and labelled as such.
- **A combiner fitted by nested CV of both models.** `deep.fusion: stack` gets the same thing
  more cheaply and still leakage-safe: one logistic regression per (repeat, fold) on the fold's
  inner-validation files, whose embeddings already exist. The pre-declared convex blend is kept
  alongside it so both combinations are on the record.
- **PDF/CDF and censoring-aware dwell-time estimators.** PSD and ACF enter as features.
- **Baseline/detrending candidate comparison with synthetic-event recovery scoring.**
- **Chunked processing.** Each recording is loaded in full (about 80 MB at 1 MHz); the memory
  check P20 refuses to run if the budget is exceeded.
- **FDTD simulation, deconvolution, free-energy analysis, volume or structure inference.**
  Out of scope pending physical parameters (design §13).
- **Self-supervised pretraining (masked reconstruction, SimCLR).** Its gate (enough unlabelled
  windows from training groups) is not met by a 135-file labelled dataset, and each method
  would have to beat its supervised counterpart to be worth the compute.
- **Automatic feature selection on the pooled matrix** (RFE, Boruta, L1 pruning). Any selection
  scored on the pooled features uses test-fold information; the ablation reports the evidence and
  the selection stays a declared choice.
- Generative augmentation, triplet mining, attention encoders, and multi-task heads: each is
  listed with its reason in `docs/LEARNING_STRATEGIES.md` §5.

## Limitations

- Results on SYNTHETIC data validate code paths. They say nothing about any experiment.
- With no replicate or session IDs, batch effects cannot be separated from the class label.
  The baseline-only shortcut probe is the available guard; run it and read it before
  believing a model.
- File-level labels over event-level signals inject label noise that no model can fix. The
  label-noise rate is reported for that reason.
- Window length, and therefore Model B, depends on this pool's event-duration distribution.
  Numbers are not comparable across datasets with different event durations.
- The default configuration fails preflight on purpose until `source_dir` and labels are set.

